# ferrigura

<!-- mdtheme:badges:start -->

[![crates.io](https://img.shields.io/crates/v/ferrigura?style=flat-square&logo=rust&label=crates.io)](https://crates.io/crates/ferrigura)
[![docs.rs](https://img.shields.io/docsrs/ferrigura?style=flat-square&logo=docsdotrs&label=docs.rs)](https://docs.rs/ferrigura)
[![CI](https://img.shields.io/github/actions/workflow/status/sebastian-software/ferrigura/ci.yml?branch=main&style=flat-square&logo=github&label=CI)](https://github.com/sebastian-software/ferrigura/actions/workflows/ci.yml)
[![License](https://img.shields.io/badge/license-MIT-blue?style=flat-square)](./LICENSE)

<!-- mdtheme:badges:end -->

ferrigura renders [Mermaid](https://github.com/mermaid-js/mermaid) diagram text to SVG in Rust, without a browser. It is a port of Mermaid 12.2.0 with all 33 of its diagram types: the same diagram text gives the same diagram, and the geometry of the output is checked against Mermaid run in Chromium.

```mermaid
flowchart LR
  text[Diagram text] --> parse[Parser] --> layout[Layout: ELK, swimlane, cose-bilkent, fcose, venn] --> svg[SVG]
```

## Using ferrigura

[crates/ferrigura/README.md](./crates/ferrigura/README.md) describes the library, [crates/ferrigura-cli/README.md](./crates/ferrigura-cli/README.md) the command-line tool, and [crates/ferrigura-node/README.md](./crates/ferrigura-node/README.md) the Node.js addon. The library and the command-line tool are on crates.io as [`ferrigura`](https://crates.io/crates/ferrigura) and [`ferrigura-cli`](https://crates.io/crates/ferrigura-cli); the standalone npm distribution is prepared in `node/` and awaits registry bootstrap.

```sh
cargo add ferrigura
```

```sh
cargo install ferrigura-cli
```

## Alternatives

Other projects render Mermaid's syntax too. Which one fits depends on where the diagrams are drawn and how close to Mermaid they must look; [the comparison](./docs/comparisons/README.md) sets them side by side, with measured sizes and render times.

| Project                                                                | A fit when                                                                                                                                                 |
| ---------------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| [Mermaid](https://mermaid.js.org/)                                     | Diagrams are drawn in a browser, with click callbacks, tooltips and the newest diagram types first. On a server it needs a headless browser (mermaid-cli). |
| [Pele](https://pele.run/)                                              | Speed matters most, in any JavaScript runtime, and a layout and style of its own are fine.                                                                 |
| [merman](https://github.com/Latias94/merman)                           | Mermaid's drawing is wanted in Rust with output beyond SVG (PNG, PDF, ASCII) or bindings such as WebAssembly and Python, measured without bundled fonts.   |
| [mermaid-rs-renderer](https://github.com/1jehuang/mermaid-rs-renderer) | A small, fast Rust renderer with a layout of its own is enough, for 23 of the diagram types.                                                               |
| ferrigura                                                              | Mermaid 12.2.0's drawing is wanted without a browser and the same on every machine, with its fonts embedded: in Rust, on the command line or in Node.      |

